data.content:
Here's what breaks in C# 13 and the fixes:

## 1. Buffer.cs — `[InlineArray]` on `record struct` is now illegal

C# 13 disallows inline arrays on record structs (they shouldn't have synthesized `Equals`/`GetHashCode` over a single field).

```csharp
[System.Runtime.CompilerServices.InlineArray(10)]
struct Buffer            // remove "record"
{
    private int _element0;
}
```

## 2. Generator.cs — `unsafe` no longer flows into iterators

C# 13 stops the `unsafe` class context from leaking into iterator methods (and their local functions). Mark the local function explicitly:

```csharp
unsafe class DataGenerator
{
    IEnumerable<int> Generate()
    {
        yield return 1;
        Process();
        static unsafe void Process()   // explicit unsafe on the local function
        {
            int* p = stackalloc int[10];
        }
    }
}
```

## 3. Resolver.cs — New implicit span conversions cause ambiguity

C# 13 adds first-class `Span`/`ReadOnlySpan` conversions, so `[]` now matches *both* overloads equally (previously `Span<object>` wasn't a candidate for an empty collection expression of `int`).

Fix by disambiguating:

```csharp
static void Main()
{
    Process(ReadOnlySpan<int>.Empty);   // or cast: Process((ReadOnlySpan<int>)[]);
}
```

---

| File | Breaking change | Fix |
|------|----------------|-----|
| Buffer.cs | `[InlineArray]` banned on record structs | Use plain `struct` |
| Generator.cs | `unsafe` context no longer inherited by iterators | Add `unsafe` to the local function |
| Resolver.cs | Implicit span conversions create overload ambiguity | Explicitly specify the target type |
